1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,391
There was a court order
to take this house.

2
00:00:02,391 --> 00:00:04,115
Not the man who broke the law —

3
00:00:04,115 --> 00:00:05,061
the house itself.

4
00:00:05,061 --> 00:00:07,118
And the family who owned it had never

5
00:00:07,118 --> 00:00:09,009
been charged with anything at all.

6
00:00:09,359 --> 00:00:11,480
This is the story of how an American

7
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,779
city could seize your home without ever

8
00:00:13,779 --> 00:00:16,018
proving you did a single thing wrong —

9
00:00:16,018 --> 00:00:17,668
how it did it to more than a

10
00:00:17,668 --> 00:00:18,788
thousand families —

11
00:00:18,788 --> 00:00:21,027
and how one house painter and three of

12
00:00:21,027 --> 00:00:23,384
his neighbors finally forced it to stop.

13
00:00:23,734 --> 00:00:26,628
Christos Sourovelis painted
houses for a living.

14
00:00:26,628 --> 00:00:28,738
For years he climbed other people's

15
00:00:28,738 --> 00:00:30,848
ladders and rolled color onto other

16
00:00:30,848 --> 00:00:33,320
people's walls, and slowly it added up to

17
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,732
something of his own: a modest two-story

18
00:00:35,732 --> 00:00:37,661
house in the northeast corner of

19
00:00:37,661 --> 00:00:39,892
Philadelphia, in a quiet neighborhood

20
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:40,857
called Somerton.

21
00:00:40,857 --> 00:00:43,268
It was worth a little over three hundred

22
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:44,293
thousand dollars.

23
00:00:44,293 --> 00:00:45,800
He and his wife, Markela,

24
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:47,790
had earned it one wall at a time.

25
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,622
It was, by every measure,

26
00:00:49,622 --> 00:00:51,045
an ordinary family home.

27
00:00:51,045 --> 00:00:53,358
And ordinary is the whole point of this

28
00:00:53,358 --> 00:00:54,058
story —

29
00:00:54,058 --> 00:00:56,489
because what happened to the Sourovelises

30
00:00:56,489 --> 00:00:58,149
was not built for criminals.

31
00:00:58,149 --> 00:01:00,354
It was built for people exactly like them.

32
00:01:00,704 --> 00:01:02,147
In March of 2014, their

33
00:01:02,147 --> 00:01:04,595
twenty-two-year-old son, Yianni, made a

34
00:01:04,595 --> 00:01:07,293
decision that would
reach far past himself.

35
00:01:07,293 --> 00:01:09,804
He sold about forty dollars of heroin to

36
00:01:09,804 --> 00:01:10,557
a stranger —

37
00:01:10,557 --> 00:01:13,067
and the stranger was a police informant.

38
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:15,389
Within days, Yianni was under arrest.

39
00:01:15,389 --> 00:01:17,272
He would later be ordered into

40
00:01:17,272 --> 00:01:19,280
court-supervised rehabilitation.

41
00:01:19,630 --> 00:01:20,486
Forty dollars.

42
00:01:20,486 --> 00:01:22,689
A small, ugly mistake by a young man

43
00:01:22,689 --> 00:01:23,389
in trouble.

44
00:01:23,389 --> 00:01:25,591
His parents hadn't even known he was

45
00:01:25,591 --> 00:01:28,500
involved with heroin until
the police told them.

46
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:31,617
And here is where you would expect the

47
00:01:31,617 --> 00:01:32,564
story to end.

48
00:01:32,564 --> 00:01:34,021
A son in the system.

49
00:01:34,021 --> 00:01:37,443
A family shaken, quietly,
the way families are.

50
00:01:37,443 --> 00:01:38,463
Life going on.

51
00:01:38,813 --> 00:01:41,190
Except the city of Philadelphia was not

52
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:43,079
finished with the Sourovelises.

53
00:01:43,079 --> 00:01:45,577
Because while everyone was looking at the

54
00:01:45,577 --> 00:01:47,771
son, the city had quietly turned its

55
00:01:47,771 --> 00:01:49,478
attention to something else.

56
00:01:49,478 --> 00:01:50,331
Not the crime.

57
00:01:50,331 --> 00:01:51,672
Not the forty dollars.

58
00:01:51,672 --> 00:01:52,372
The house.

59
00:01:52,631 --> 00:01:53,810
On the eighth of May,

60
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:56,055
prosecutors walked into a courtroom with

61
00:01:56,055 --> 00:01:57,796
no one from the family present,

62
00:01:57,796 --> 00:01:59,873
and walked out with an order to seize

63
00:01:59,873 --> 00:02:01,501
and seal the Sourovelis home.

64
00:02:01,851 --> 00:02:03,092
There was no warning.

65
00:02:03,092 --> 00:02:04,333
There was no hearing.

66
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:06,105
There was a knock at the door,

67
00:02:06,105 --> 00:02:08,528
and an instruction that sounds impossible

68
00:02:08,528 --> 00:02:10,892
for a home someone actually owns: pack a

69
00:02:10,892 --> 00:02:11,896
bag, and get out.

70
00:02:11,896 --> 00:02:13,728
The door Christos had paid for,

71
00:02:13,728 --> 00:02:16,033
in the neighborhood he'd chosen to grow

72
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:18,101
old in, was sealed shut by the same

73
00:02:18,101 --> 00:02:19,637
city he paid his taxes to.

74
00:02:19,987 --> 00:02:20,687
Now —

75
00:02:20,687 --> 00:02:23,732
stop and ask the question
the Sourovelises asked.

76
00:02:23,732 --> 00:02:26,280
How can a government take your house when

77
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:28,455
you have been charged with nothing?

78
00:02:28,455 --> 00:02:30,257
Christos hadn't broken a law.

79
00:02:30,257 --> 00:02:31,997
Markela hadn't broken a law.

80
00:02:31,997 --> 00:02:33,037
How is this even legal?

81
00:02:33,387 --> 00:02:35,970
The answer is a strange and powerful tool

82
00:02:35,970 --> 00:02:37,482
called civil forfeiture.

83
00:02:37,482 --> 00:02:39,309
In an ordinary criminal case,

84
00:02:39,309 --> 00:02:41,325
the government accuses a person,

85
00:02:41,325 --> 00:02:43,782
and it has to prove that person's guilt

86
00:02:43,782 --> 00:02:45,420
beyond a reasonable doubt.

87
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,003
Civil forfeiture flips the whole thing on

88
00:02:48,003 --> 00:02:48,703
its head.

89
00:02:48,703 --> 00:02:51,286
The government doesn't accuse the person.

90
00:02:51,286 --> 00:02:52,798
It accuses the property.

91
00:02:52,798 --> 00:02:55,318
The house itself becomes the defendant —

92
00:02:55,318 --> 00:02:57,334
as if the walls and the roof had

93
00:02:57,334 --> 00:02:58,468
committed a crime.

94
00:02:58,468 --> 00:03:00,169
And property has no rights.

95
00:03:00,169 --> 00:03:02,436
A house cannot be presumed innocent.

96
00:03:02,436 --> 00:03:04,704
A house is not entitled to a lawyer.

97
00:03:04,704 --> 00:03:05,460
The owners —

98
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:07,035
the actual human beings —

99
00:03:07,035 --> 00:03:08,736
are almost an afterthought.

100
00:03:08,736 --> 00:03:11,130
And the burden of proof flips with it.

101
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:13,461
In a criminal trial, the state has to

102
00:03:13,461 --> 00:03:15,099
prove that you are guilty.

103
00:03:15,099 --> 00:03:17,493
In civil forfeiture, you often have to

104
00:03:17,493 --> 00:03:20,139
prove that your own property is innocent —

105
00:03:20,139 --> 00:03:22,407
to convince a court that your house,

106
00:03:22,407 --> 00:03:24,738
your car, or your cash had nothing to

107
00:03:24,738 --> 00:03:27,258
do with a crime, or watch the government

108
00:03:27,258 --> 00:03:27,958
keep it.

109
00:03:27,979 --> 00:03:30,399
In fact, these cases are given names that

110
00:03:30,399 --> 00:03:32,760
sound absurd once you say them out loud:

111
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,530
the government versus a house.

112
00:03:34,530 --> 00:03:36,183
The government versus a car.

113
00:03:36,183 --> 00:03:38,426
The government versus a stack of cash.

114
00:03:38,426 --> 00:03:40,668
Read a civil-forfeiture docket and you

115
00:03:40,668 --> 00:03:42,852
will find the state suing an object —

116
00:03:42,852 --> 00:03:45,272
because an object cannot afford a lawyer,

117
00:03:45,272 --> 00:03:46,924
and an object can never say,

118
00:03:46,924 --> 00:03:48,459
"but I did nothing wrong."

119
00:03:48,809 --> 00:03:50,956
So the Sourovelises did the only thing

120
00:03:50,956 --> 00:03:51,656
they could.

121
00:03:51,656 --> 00:03:53,520
For about a week, they slept on a

122
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:54,537
relative's couch —

123
00:03:54,537 --> 00:03:56,853
a family made homeless in their own city,

124
00:03:56,853 --> 00:03:58,943
while the government held the deed to

125
00:03:58,943 --> 00:04:00,412
their home like a hostage.

126
00:04:00,412 --> 00:04:02,332
They had dropped their son off for

127
00:04:02,332 --> 00:04:04,422
court-ordered rehab that very day; by

128
00:04:04,422 --> 00:04:07,190
nightfall they had nowhere
of their own to sleep.

129
00:04:07,190 --> 00:04:09,337
Everything they owned sat on the other

130
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:11,518
side of a door the city had sealed shut.

131
00:04:11,868 --> 00:04:14,261
They were finally allowed back inside on a

132
00:04:14,261 --> 00:04:15,229
single condition.

133
00:04:15,229 --> 00:04:17,109
Christos had to sign an agreement

134
00:04:17,109 --> 00:04:19,615
promising to bar his
own son from the house.

135
00:04:19,615 --> 00:04:20,527
Read that again.

136
00:04:20,527 --> 00:04:21,837
To get their home back,

137
00:04:21,837 --> 00:04:24,001
these parents had to give up their son

138
00:04:24,001 --> 00:04:24,701
at the door.

139
00:04:25,035 --> 00:04:27,618
And if they wanted to actually fight the

140
00:04:27,618 --> 00:04:28,318
seizure —

141
00:04:28,318 --> 00:04:30,643
to argue they'd done nothing wrong —

142
00:04:30,643 --> 00:04:32,774
there was a place they had to go.

143
00:04:32,774 --> 00:04:34,582
It was called Courtroom 478,

144
00:04:34,582 --> 00:04:37,552
on an upper floor of
Philadelphia's City Hall.

145
00:04:37,552 --> 00:04:40,070
But calling it a courtroom is generous.

146
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:42,395
There was no judge in Courtroom 478.

147
00:04:42,395 --> 00:04:44,720
The proceedings were run by the very

148
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:47,367
prosecutors trying to take your property.

149
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:49,886
There were no court-appointed lawyers —

150
00:04:49,886 --> 00:04:51,629
no right to counsel at all.

151
00:04:51,629 --> 00:04:53,502
Owners were handed forms and,

152
00:04:53,502 --> 00:04:56,214
more often than not, encouraged simply not

153
00:04:56,214 --> 00:04:56,914
to fight.

154
00:04:56,914 --> 00:04:58,593
And if you missed a date —

155
00:04:58,593 --> 00:05:00,466
if you couldn't get off work,

156
00:05:00,466 --> 00:05:02,532
if the letter never reached you,

157
00:05:02,532 --> 00:05:04,211
if you didn't understand —

158
00:05:04,211 --> 00:05:06,729
you could lose your home automatically,

159
00:05:06,729 --> 00:05:07,429
by default.

160
00:05:07,552 --> 00:05:10,167
This was not a place
where justice was weighed.

161
00:05:10,167 --> 00:05:12,338
It was a collection department with the

162
00:05:12,338 --> 00:05:14,007
seal of the state on the door.

163
00:05:14,357 --> 00:05:16,935
And here is the part that should genuinely

164
00:05:16,935 --> 00:05:19,882
stop you: the Sourovelis
family was not unusual.

165
00:05:19,882 --> 00:05:21,478
They were not singled out.

166
00:05:21,478 --> 00:05:23,810
They were simply one address on a very

167
00:05:23,810 --> 00:05:24,510
long list.

168
00:05:24,510 --> 00:05:27,027
It could just as easily have been yours —

169
00:05:27,027 --> 00:05:28,807
not because you did anything,

170
00:05:28,807 --> 00:05:31,263
but because someone under your roof did,

171
00:05:31,263 --> 00:05:33,780
or because an officer decided the cash in

172
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:35,621
your pocket looked suspicious.

173
00:05:35,621 --> 00:05:38,015
The typical target was never a criminal

174
00:05:38,015 --> 00:05:38,715
enterprise.

175
00:05:38,715 --> 00:05:41,109
It was an ordinary household that could

176
00:05:41,109 --> 00:05:42,828
not afford to hire a lawyer,

177
00:05:42,828 --> 00:05:45,222
take days off work, and come back again

178
00:05:45,222 --> 00:05:47,432
and again to a hearing built to make

179
00:05:47,432 --> 00:05:48,132
them give up.

180
00:05:48,469 --> 00:05:51,062
Between 2002 and 2014, the city of

181
00:05:51,062 --> 00:05:54,265
Philadelphia used civil forfeiture to take

182
00:05:54,265 --> 00:05:56,629
more than twelve hundred homes.

183
00:05:56,629 --> 00:05:59,298
More than thirty-five hundred cars.

184
00:05:59,298 --> 00:06:01,967
Over fifty million dollars in cash.

185
00:06:01,967 --> 00:06:04,789
And when you look at the typical cash

186
00:06:04,789 --> 00:06:05,489
seizure —

187
00:06:05,489 --> 00:06:08,616
not the headline cases, the typical one —

188
00:06:08,616 --> 00:06:12,339
it was about a hundred
and seventy-eight dollars.

189
00:06:12,689 --> 00:06:14,652
Sit with that number for a second.

190
00:06:14,652 --> 00:06:16,731
A hundred and seventy-eight dollars.

191
00:06:16,731 --> 00:06:19,157
This machine was never built to bring down

192
00:06:19,157 --> 00:06:19,857
drug lords.

193
00:06:19,857 --> 00:06:21,878
It was built to take small amounts,

194
00:06:21,878 --> 00:06:23,149
from ordinary people —

195
00:06:23,149 --> 00:06:25,516
the people with the least ability to hire

196
00:06:25,516 --> 00:06:27,249
a lawyer, take a day off work,

197
00:06:27,249 --> 00:06:28,339
and fight City Hall.

198
00:06:28,689 --> 00:06:29,389
But why?

199
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:31,511
Why would a city grind so hard on

200
00:06:31,511 --> 00:06:32,925
people with so little?

201
00:06:32,925 --> 00:06:35,305
Follow the money, and the whole thing

202
00:06:35,305 --> 00:06:36,212
snaps into focus.

203
00:06:36,562 --> 00:06:38,973
Under this system, the cash and the cars

204
00:06:38,973 --> 00:06:41,445
and the houses the police and prosecutors

205
00:06:41,445 --> 00:06:43,796
seized flowed right back into their own

206
00:06:43,796 --> 00:06:44,496
budgets —

207
00:06:44,496 --> 00:06:46,546
nearly six million dollars a year,

208
00:06:46,546 --> 00:06:49,018
money that helped pay the salaries of the

209
00:06:49,018 --> 00:06:50,826
very people doing the seizing.

210
00:06:50,826 --> 00:06:53,298
Over a single decade, Philadelphia pulled

211
00:06:53,298 --> 00:06:55,589
in more this way than Brooklyn and Los

212
00:06:55,589 --> 00:06:56,614
Angeles combined.

213
00:06:56,614 --> 00:06:59,086
The incentive was built into the machine:

214
00:06:59,086 --> 00:07:01,316
the more they took, the more they had

215
00:07:01,316 --> 00:07:02,016
to spend.

216
00:07:02,016 --> 00:07:04,669
And nothing about that
pointed toward mercy.

217
00:07:04,669 --> 00:07:06,719
No warrant was needed to begin it,

218
00:07:06,719 --> 00:07:09,251
and no conviction was needed to finish it.

219
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:11,421
A prosecutor could move against your

220
00:07:11,421 --> 00:07:13,893
property on a Monday, and if you couldn't

221
00:07:13,893 --> 00:07:16,123
work through the paperwork perfectly,

222
00:07:16,123 --> 00:07:17,932
it could be gone by the end of

223
00:07:17,932 --> 00:07:18,632
the month —

224
00:07:18,632 --> 00:07:21,164
while the office that took it counted your

225
00:07:21,164 --> 00:07:22,259
loss as its own revenue.

226
00:07:22,609 --> 00:07:24,940
It had grown so brazen that even the

227
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:27,594
Supreme Court had started to take notice.

228
00:07:27,594 --> 00:07:30,055
In 2017, Justice Clarence Thomas wrote

229
00:07:30,055 --> 00:07:32,385
that civil forfeiture had produced —

230
00:07:32,385 --> 00:07:33,097
his words —

231
00:07:33,097 --> 00:07:35,752
"egregious and well-chronicled abuses," a

232
00:07:35,752 --> 00:07:38,471
system that "frequently target[s] the poor

233
00:07:38,471 --> 00:07:40,867
and other groups least able to defend

234
00:07:40,867 --> 00:07:43,262
their interests." Two years later the

235
00:07:43,262 --> 00:07:45,722
Court would go further still: in 2019,

236
00:07:45,722 --> 00:07:47,341
in a case out of Indiana,

237
00:07:47,341 --> 00:07:48,636
the justices ruled —

238
00:07:48,636 --> 00:07:49,478
unanimously —

239
00:07:49,478 --> 00:07:52,067
that the Constitution's ban on excessive

240
00:07:52,067 --> 00:07:54,463
fines applies to seizures like these,

241
00:07:54,463 --> 00:07:55,434
in every state.

242
00:07:55,434 --> 00:07:57,441
The tide was beginning to turn.

243
00:07:57,441 --> 00:08:00,225
But in 2014, none of
that had happened yet.

244
00:08:00,575 --> 00:08:03,202
But you cannot appeal to the Supreme Court

245
00:08:03,202 --> 00:08:04,704
from a relative's couch.

246
00:08:04,704 --> 00:08:07,206
So the Sourovelises did something bigger

247
00:08:07,206 --> 00:08:08,582
than saving one house.

248
00:08:08,582 --> 00:08:10,959
They joined with a public-interest law

249
00:08:10,959 --> 00:08:12,961
firm, the Institute for Justice,

250
00:08:12,961 --> 00:08:15,463
and filed the very first class action in

251
00:08:15,463 --> 00:08:16,776
that firm's history —

252
00:08:16,776 --> 00:08:18,716
a lawsuit on behalf of everyone

253
00:08:18,716 --> 00:08:20,592
Philadelphia had done this to.

254
00:08:20,592 --> 00:08:23,032
Christos Sourovelis put his name at the

255
00:08:23,032 --> 00:08:25,659
top of it, alongside three others the city

256
00:08:25,659 --> 00:08:27,411
had taken from: Doila Welch,

257
00:08:27,411 --> 00:08:29,600
Norys Hernandez, and Nassir Geiger.

258
00:08:29,950 --> 00:08:32,214
The case wasn't titled around the drugs,

259
00:08:32,214 --> 00:08:33,911
or the forty dollars, anymore.

260
00:08:33,911 --> 00:08:36,118
It was one family, and their neighbors,

261
00:08:36,118 --> 00:08:37,646
against the entire machine.

262
00:08:37,646 --> 00:08:40,023
And now the question was no longer whether

263
00:08:40,023 --> 00:08:42,117
one painter could win back one house.

264
00:08:42,117 --> 00:08:44,380
It was whether an American city could be

265
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:45,229
forced to stop.

266
00:08:45,579 --> 00:08:46,746
It took four years.

267
00:08:46,746 --> 00:08:48,897
Four years of hearings and filings,

268
00:08:48,897 --> 00:08:51,416
of a city with far deeper pockets betting

269
00:08:51,416 --> 00:08:53,690
the family would eventually give up —

270
00:08:53,690 --> 00:08:55,656
the way almost everyone gave up.

271
00:08:56,006 --> 00:08:58,372
The Sourovelises got their own home free

272
00:08:58,372 --> 00:08:59,615
within about a year —

273
00:08:59,615 --> 00:09:02,040
the city quietly dropped its case against

274
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:03,815
their house, and they kept it.

275
00:09:03,815 --> 00:09:05,530
That could have been the end.

276
00:09:05,530 --> 00:09:08,252
They could have taken
the win and walked away.

277
00:09:08,252 --> 00:09:10,263
They didn't drop the class action.

278
00:09:10,613 --> 00:09:12,900
And in 2018, Philadelphia gave up the

279
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:13,827
machine itself.

280
00:09:13,827 --> 00:09:16,238
The city signed federal agreements that

281
00:09:16,238 --> 00:09:18,834
dismantled the program at its foundations.

282
00:09:18,834 --> 00:09:21,924
No more seizing homes over
simple drug possession.

283
00:09:21,924 --> 00:09:24,458
No more taking small amounts of cash from

284
00:09:24,458 --> 00:09:26,683
people who were never even arrested.

285
00:09:26,683 --> 00:09:27,487
Real judges —

286
00:09:27,487 --> 00:09:28,538
not prosecutors —

287
00:09:28,538 --> 00:09:30,206
would now run the hearings.

288
00:09:30,206 --> 00:09:32,741
The police and the district attorney were

289
00:09:32,741 --> 00:09:35,213
banned from spending forfeiture money on

290
00:09:35,213 --> 00:09:37,685
their own budgets, cutting the incentive

291
00:09:37,685 --> 00:09:38,674
out at the root.

292
00:09:38,674 --> 00:09:41,270
And from now on, the government would have

293
00:09:41,270 --> 00:09:43,866
to prove that an owner actually knew about

294
00:09:43,866 --> 00:09:46,153
a crime before taking their property.

295
00:09:46,153 --> 00:09:47,575
The city also created a

296
00:09:47,575 --> 00:09:50,109
three-million-dollar fund to pay back the

297
00:09:50,109 --> 00:09:51,716
people it had taken from —

298
00:09:51,716 --> 00:09:54,065
with those never convicted of anything

299
00:09:54,065 --> 00:09:56,413
able to recover the full value of what

300
00:09:56,413 --> 00:09:57,155
they'd lost.

301
00:09:57,155 --> 00:09:58,577
It was, in plain terms,

302
00:09:58,577 --> 00:09:59,318
a surrender.

303
00:09:59,318 --> 00:10:01,420
The city was not just settling one

304
00:10:01,420 --> 00:10:03,583
family's lawsuit; it was admitting,

305
00:10:03,583 --> 00:10:05,870
in writing, that for years it had run

306
00:10:05,870 --> 00:10:08,590
a system the Constitution
could not defend —

307
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:10,753
and agreeing never to run it again.

308
00:10:11,103 --> 00:10:12,560
And Courtroom 478 —

309
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:14,400
the room with no judge —

310
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:15,933
was closed for good.

311
00:10:16,283 --> 00:10:17,897
Remember how this began.

312
00:10:17,897 --> 00:10:19,444
Not with a guilty man —

313
00:10:19,444 --> 00:10:20,789
with a guilty house.

314
00:10:20,789 --> 00:10:22,807
A family who had done nothing,

315
00:10:22,807 --> 00:10:24,959
told to pack a bag and leave the

316
00:10:24,959 --> 00:10:26,035
home they owned.

317
00:10:26,385 --> 00:10:27,799
They did nothing wrong.

318
00:10:27,799 --> 00:10:29,521
And for a while, in America,

319
00:10:29,521 --> 00:10:31,919
that wasn't enough to keep their house.

320
00:10:31,919 --> 00:10:34,256
Because the entire promise of American

321
00:10:34,256 --> 00:10:36,346
justice runs in one direction: the

322
00:10:36,346 --> 00:10:38,068
government has to prove it —

323
00:10:38,068 --> 00:10:39,851
before it takes your freedom,

324
00:10:39,851 --> 00:10:41,573
before it takes your things,

325
00:10:41,573 --> 00:10:44,155
before it takes the roof over your family.

326
00:10:44,155 --> 00:10:46,615
In Philadelphia, for years, that promise

327
00:10:46,615 --> 00:10:47,476
ran backwards.

328
00:10:47,476 --> 00:10:49,812
They took first, and left you to prove

329
00:10:49,812 --> 00:10:50,673
your way home.

330
00:10:51,023 --> 00:10:53,132
It took a house painter and three of

331
00:10:53,132 --> 00:10:55,767
his neighbors to turn
it right side up again.

332
00:10:55,767 --> 00:10:57,876
Christos Sourovelis never set out to

333
00:10:57,876 --> 00:10:58,755
change the law.

334
00:10:58,755 --> 00:11:00,512
He only wanted his house back.

335
00:11:00,512 --> 00:11:02,621
But by refusing to walk away once he

336
00:11:02,621 --> 00:11:04,964
had it, he helped take the whole machine

337
00:11:04,964 --> 00:11:05,664
apart —

338
00:11:05,664 --> 00:11:08,416
for everyone still standing
in line behind him.

339
00:11:08,416 --> 00:11:10,642
The reforms he won did not only return

340
00:11:10,642 --> 00:11:11,462
his own house.

341
00:11:11,462 --> 00:11:13,922
They rewrote the rules for every family in

342
00:11:13,922 --> 00:11:16,207
the city who might someday hear a knock

343
00:11:16,207 --> 00:11:18,081
at the door and be told that the

344
00:11:18,081 --> 00:11:19,663
home they had paid for was,

345
00:11:19,663 --> 00:11:20,837
somehow, now the accused.

346
00:11:21,187 --> 00:11:23,342
If you didn't know your own government

347
00:11:23,342 --> 00:11:24,192
could do this —

348
00:11:24,192 --> 00:11:26,063
hit like, so that more people do.
